WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.140
Many core protections are configuration-driven.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:25.560
Authentication settings, session secrets, cookie attributes, JWT issuers and audiences, CORS origins, CSRF behavior, TLS and proxy settings, rate limits, request size limits, upload limits, logging levels, error verbosity, and admin feature toggles all influence security behavior.

3
00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:30.300
Defaults should be safe for production or require explicit production configuration.

4
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.240
A missing setting should not quietly turn into a development convenience.

5
00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.800
Express-style applications make the tradeoffs visible.

6
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:42.140
A wildcard CORS value may be convenient locally, but risky in production.

7
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.680
A cookie without Secure may work during HTTP development, but it is wrong behind production HTTPS.

8
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:53.140
A debug route may help an engineer diagnose locally, but expose internals if deployed.

9
00:00:53.240 --> 00:01:05.640
Verbose errors, mock authentication, test accounts, relaxed cookie settings, broad proxy trust, and disabled request limits should be controlled, reviewed, and visible.

10
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:12.480
Configuration should distinguish public configuration, sensitive configuration, and secrets.

11
00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.580
A public frontend API base URL is different from a signing key.

12
00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.720
A feature flag name is different from a database password.

13
00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:25.180
Settings that weaken protection should require explicit justification and operational visibility.

14
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:32.400
Secure defaults reduce the chance that one forgotten value turns production into a development-shaped system.
